{
  "result": "The first rain after a long summer drought does not arrive as weather; it arrives as permission. Windows open, dust loosens from leaves, and the whole neighborhood seems to exhale at once.\n\nA child leaves a paper boat in the gutter, giving it a fleet and a destination. Across the street, a baker carries trays beneath an awning, laughing when the wind sprays flour from his sleeves. Even the traffic changes its voice. Tires whisper over wet pavement; horns sound less like impatience than startled birds.\n\nI used to think a useful day had to produce something measurable: a finished task, a checked box, proof that time had been spent correctly. But rain makes a gentler argument. It asks nothing of the people watching from doorways. It simply changes what is possible for a little while.\n\nThe air cools. A forgotten garden brightens. Someone who was hurrying slows down to look at a puddle holding the sky upside down. Perhaps attention is its own kind of shelter. We step beneath it, briefly, and notice that ordinary things have been waiting patiently to become remarkable.\n\nWhen the clouds move on, the paper boat will be gone, and the baker will sweep flour from the floor. The day will resume its errands. Still, something will remain: a small permission to pause, offered without ceremony by water, light, and the people sharing one wet street. Tomorrow, we may forget the lesson, but the pavement will remember how briefly it shone under everyone\u2019s feet.",
